messages:
maria: quick heads-up — our integration tests are flapping. lib-client treats responses with no body as text/plain, so JSON.parse throws. Seen this on /v1/models/:id/api-preview and a couple of embedding routes. anyone touched content-type behavior recently?

kevin: i pushed a tiny change to the handler last week to shortcut empty model metadata responses — might be related. i intended to return an empty body with 204, but middleware may be adding text/plain.

sara: hmm, SDK expects application/json for 200s and treats 204 as an empty success. if the server returns 200 with text/plain it's a break. which endpoints are returning 200 vs 204?

maria: both. sometimes 200 + "" body, sometimes 204. client stack: fetch -> if content-type includes json then parse else return text. failing test shows content-type: text/plain; charset=utf-8 and empty body.

kevin: i think the issue is our fallback that sets default content-type from express when body is empty. quick fix: set res.type('application/json') explicitly when serializing, and for 204 ensure no content-type header at all.

nate: we should pick a single behavior for backward compatibility. options: 
- always 200 + JSON object (e.g., {}) even when nothing to return — safest for clients that always parse JSON
- use 204 for genuinely empty responses and update SDK to handle it
- allow null payloads and document schema to accept nulls for optional fields

sara: preference is to avoid forcing SDKs to special-case parsing unless necessary. Many customers embed SDKs that assume json. I'd vote for consistent application/json responses for 200, and use 204 only for endpoints designed to return no resource.

maria: agree. For these model metadata endpoints, we should return 200 + {} or {"metadata": null} according to schema. We must also ensure Content-Type header is accurately set.

kevin: ok i'll revert the middleware fallback and add explicit header set in the unified serializer. patch plan:
```js
// in response serializer
if (body === undefined || body === null) {
  // prefer stable JSON for these endpoints
  body = {};
}
res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json; charset=utf-8');
res.statusCode = status || 200;
res.end(JSON.stringify(body));
```

maria: that looks good. two followups: update JSON schema to allow nullable for fields that can be missing, and add an integration test that asserts content-type for empty payloads.

nate: suggested JSON schema snippet we can add to repo (schema repo/contract):
```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "id": { "type": "string" },
    "metadata": { "oneOf": [ { "type": "object" }, { "type": "null" } ] }
  },
  "required": ["id"]
}
```

sara: also add a small note in CHANGELOG / API-compatibility that we are standardizing on application/json for 200 and using 204 only for no-resource responses. we should also call out that nullable object fields are modeled with oneOf/object|null so older clients that expect objects still validate.

kevin: i'll open a PR with the serializer fix + unit + integration test. PR will include:
- explicit content-type set
- behavior: null/undefined -> {} for these endpoints (configurable per-route)
- test asserting Content-Type and that SDK can parse successfully

maria: thanks. sara can you add SDK change to tolerate 204? just in case we do use it elsewhere. small patch: treat 204 as success without parsing.

sara: yep can do. will add a tiny guard: if response.status === 204 return null else parse. will also bump SDK minor and add migration note.

deploy-bot: deploy queued: serializer-fix branch -> staging (ETA ~20m) :rocket:

maria: assigned kevin to the server fix, sara on SDK patch, nate to update schema docs. i'll follow up in the PR with exact endpoints and testcases. thanks all :thumbsup:
